, "n_D_cm2": 8000000000.0}, "locator": "Eq. 3 and Eq. 5 at E_L = 1.96 eV"}, {"inputs": {"I_D_over_I_G": 2.0, "laser_nm": 785.0}, "expected": {"L_D_nm": 18.574, "n_D_cm2": 91000000000.0}, "tolerance": {"L_D_nm": 0.3, "n_D_cm2": 8000000000.0}, "locator": "Eq. 3 and Eq. 5 at E_L = 1.58 eV"}]}, {"name": "graphene_strain_doping_from_G_2D", "material": "graphene", "aliases": ["monolayer graphene", "single-layer graphene"], "inputs": {"omega_G_cm1": "G peak position in cm-1", "omega_2D_cm1": "2D peak position in cm-1"}, "optional_inputs": {"laser_nm": "excitation wavelength in nm (must be 514 nm)", "strain_type": "'uniaxial' (default) or 'biaxial'; sets the G shift per % strain"}, "outputs": {"delta_omega_G_strain_cm1": "G shift along the strain direction e_T", "delta_omega_G_doping_cm1": "G shift along the hole-doping direction e_H", "strain_percent": "strain in %, tensile positive", "doping_type": "'hole' or 'undetermined (low density)'", "a_cm1": "length along e_T", "b_cm1": "length along e_H"}, "equation": "OP = (omega_G - 1581.6, omega_2D - 2676.9) = a e_T + b e_H, with unit vectors e_T along slope d(omega_2D)/d(omega_G) = 2.2 (strain) and e_H along slope 0.70 (hole doping). Strain from the G part of a e_T: epsilon [%] = delta_omega_G_strain / (-23.5) uniaxial or / (-69.1) biaxial.", "validity": {"laser_nm": [513.0, 516.0], "sample": "single-layer graphene", "doping": "holes only (the paper excludes electron doping)", "omega_G_cm1": [1560.0, 1610.0], "omega_2D_cm1": [2620.0, 2720.0]}, "source": {"citation": "Lee, Ahn, Shim, Lee, Ryu 2012, Nat. Commun. 3, 1024", "locator": "origin (1581.6, 2676.9) p. 4; unit vectors e_T (slope 2.2) and e_H (slope 0.70) p. 5; G strain sensitivities in the Fig. 3 legend; 514 nm limit p. 9 of arXiv 1209.0234", "doi": null, "arxiv": "1209.0234"}, "caveats": ["Carrier density is not converted to cm-2: the source maps G shift to n along a nonlinear measured trajectory, not a linear rate (Lee 2012, Fig. 3 legend).", "Near the origin (|n| about 1e12 cm-2) the decomposition is ambiguous; errors up to 0.03 % strain and 1.5e12 cm-2 (Lee 2012, pp. 5 to 6).", "Strain sensitivities -23.5 (uniaxial) and -69.1 +- 3.4 cm-1/% (biaxial) are quoted by Lee 2012 from other references (Fig. 3 legend); the strain type must be known."], "self_tests": [{"inputs": {"omega_G_cm1": 1581.6, "omega_2D_cm1": 2676.9, "laser_nm": 514.5}, "expected": {"strain_percent": 0.0, "delta_omega_G_doping_cm1": 0.0}, "tolerance": {"strain_percent": 1e-09, "delta_omega_G_doping_cm1": 1e-09}, "locator": "p. 4: freestanding graphene F1, strain-free and nearly undoped, is the origin"}, {"inputs": {"omega_G_cm1": 1591.5, "omega_2D_cm1": 2693.0, "laser_nm": 514.5}, "expected": {"doping_type": "hole"}, "tolerance": {}, "locator": "p. 7: sample K4 after 400 C, (1591.5, 2693), 'strong hole doping'"}, {"inputs": {"omega_G_cm1": 1598.4, "omega_2D_cm1": 2698.0, "laser_nm": 514.5}, "expected": {"doping_type": "hole"}, "tolerance": {}, "locator": "p. 7: sample K4 after 500 C, (1598.4, 2698), 'strong hole doping'"}]}, {"name": "graphene_layers_from_2D_shape", "material": "graphene", "aliases": ["monolayer graphene", "single-layer graphene"], "inputs": {"n_2D_components": "number of Lorentzian components needed to fit the 2D band (1, 2 or 4)"}, "optional_inputs": {"I_2D_over_I_G": "ratio of the 2D and G peak heights", "laser_nm": "excitation wavelength in nm"}, "outputs": {"layer_class": "'monolayer', 'bilayer' or 'bulk graphite (or more than 5 layers)'", "confidence": "high, moderate or low"}, "equation": "Line-shape lookup (Ferrari 2006, p. 2): one sharp 2D component about 4 times the G height -> monolayer; four components (2D1B, 2D1A, 2D2A, 2D2B) -> bilayer; two components 2D1, 2D2 at about 1/4 and 1/2 of the G height -> bulk graphite.", "validity": {"n_2D_components": [1, 2, 4], "laser_nm": [510.0, 640.0], "sample": "Bernal-stacked graphene; turbostratic graphite also shows a single (twice as wide) 2D peak"}, "source": {"citation": "Ferrari et al. 2006, Phys. Rev. Lett. 97, 187401", "locator": "p. 2 (single sharp 2D peak in graphene, 4 components in bilayer, 2 in bulk graphite) and Fig. 2 of arXiv cond-mat/0606284", "doi": null, "arxiv": "cond-mat/0606284"}, "caveats": ["Spectra of more than 5 layers are hardly distinguishable from bulk graphite (Ferrari 2006, p. 2); 3 to 5 layers are not classified by this relation.", "Turbostratic graphite has a single 2D peak with FWHM about 50 cm-1, almost double that of graphene (Ferrari 2006, p. 3); check the width when one component is found."], "self_tests": [{"inputs": {"n_2D_components": 1, "I_2D_over_I_G": 4.0}, "expected": {"layer_class": "monolayer", "confidence": "high"}, "tolerance": {}, "locator": "p. 2: 'a single, sharp 2D peak in graphene, roughly 4 times more intense than the G peak'"}, {"inputs": {"n_2D_components": 4}, "expected": {"layer_class": "bilayer"}, "tolerance": {}, "locator": "p. 2 and Fig. 2e: bilayer 2D has 4 components"}, {"inputs": {"n_2D_components": 2}, "expected": {"layer_class": "bulk graphite (or more than 5 layers)"}, "tolerance": {}, "locator": "p. 2: 'The 2D peak in bulk graphite consists of two components 2D1 and 2D2'"}]}]}
